irst_name},=0A=0AA correct AI answer can still be a failed workflow. The wo=
rk closes only when=0Athe next operator, planner, quality lead, buyer, or w=
arehouse leader receives=0Athe required artifact, in the required format, w=
ith the evidence, ownership,=0Aacceptance rule, and fallback needed to act.=
 If a person still has to rebuild=0Athe work after the model responds, the =
workflow has produced an answer but not a=0Adelivery.=0A=0AThis issue moves=
 the question from "Was the answer correct=3F" to "Can the next=0Aperson ac=
t without rebuilding the work=3F" At the end, a practical field tool that=
=0Aturns one bounded workflow into a usable delivery contract.=0A=0ABill=0A=
=0A=C2=A0=0A=0ATHE SHIFT=0A=0A=0AFrom Answer Quality to Delivery Quality.=
=0A=0AThe hidden cost is the last manual mile.=0A=0AManufacturing and distr=
ibution teams do not act on prose alone. The useful=0Aresult may be a relea=
se-ready production schedule, a quality-disposition packet,=0Aa supplier-ex=
ception record, a work instruction, an audit trail, or a=0Atransaction in t=
he required system format. If a person still has to translate an=0AAI respo=
nse into that artifact, reconstruct its evidence, find its owner, or=0Adeci=
de what happens when it fails, the workflow has produced an answer but not =
a=0Adelivery.=0A=0AThe work of turning the response into an accepted artifa=
ct and routing it to the=0Aaccountable person is the last manual mile. Inte=
rpretation, reformatting,=0Aevidence gathering, routing, approval, exceptio=
n handling, and verification=0Aafter the model responds. The model is one l=
ayer. The delivery is the entire=0Asystem.=0A=0AThis is not only an editori=
al framing. NIST's Artificial Intelligence for=0AManufacturing=0A[https://w=
ww.nist.gov/programs-projects/artificial-intelligence-ai-manufacturing]=0Ap=
rogram evaluates AI-generated engineering artifacts on completeness, clarit=
y,=0Atraceability, operator understanding, and interoperability, and works =
through=0Avalidation diagrams, functional flows, and audit trails linked to=
 operator=0Ainputs, with production scheduling and process control named as=
 pilot areas. Its=0Aroadmap for AI and machine learning in smart manufactur=
ing=0A[https://www.nist.gov/publications/2026-roadmap-artificial-intelligen=
ce-and-machine-learning-smart-manufacturing]=0Aputs data management, integr=
ation with heterogeneous sensing and control=0Asystems, and trustworthy, ex=
plainable, reliable operation at the center of the=0Adeployment problem. Th=
e criteria describe the delivery, not the answer.=0A=0AThree recognizable A=
I workflow outputs=0A=0A=C2=B7 Production schedule. The planner receives a =
recommended sequence reorder, but=0Athe artifact must be import-ready, incl=
ude change summary and reason for each=0Areordered operation, attach the so=
urce constraints, show customer impact for=0Aaffected orders, and validate =
against the ERP before release.=0A=0A=C2=B7 Disposition record. Quality rec=
eives an AI recommendation to scrap, rework, or=0Arelease with conditions, =
but the record must include reference photos, test=0Adata, audit trail, acc=
ountable approver, escalation trigger if rework time=0Aexceeds threshold, a=
nd archive proof that the decision was logged and verified.=0A=0A=C2=B7 Sup=
plier exception. Procurement receives a sourcing recommendation, but it=0Am=
ust be routed to the category manager, include approved-vendor status and=
=0Apricing tier, attach the exception justification and price comparison, a=
nd=0Aretain the last three quotes for audit.=0A=0AThe operational test of A=
I in a real workflow is not whether the answer is=0Acorrect. It is whether =
the next person has the artifact, evidence, destination,=0Aowner, acceptanc=
e rule, and fallback needed to act. The difference is the=0Adistance from "=
here is what I found" to "here is what you can do with it."=0A=0ADelivering=
 an answer is step one. Delivering an artifact is the workflow.=0A=0AOpen t=
he 7-field AI Delivery Contract=0A[https://www.colonyspark.com/tools/ai-del=
ivery-contract/=3Futm_source=3Doperations_brief&utm_medium=3Demail&utm_camp=
aign=3Dai_delivery_contract&utm_content=3Dshift_button]=0A=0AComplete the a=
ssessment and leave with a delivery specification you can copy or=0Aprint a=
nd hand to the process owner, IT team, ERP partner, systems integrator,=0Ao=
r workflow vendor.=0A=0A=C2=A0=0A=0AFROM THE FLOOR=0A=0A=0AAgents work thro=
ugh approved workflows and verify the result.=0A=0ARuss Ford, Honeywell Tec=
hnologies, on closed-loop decision-making on the factory=0Afloor=0A=0A=E2=
=80=9CAgents can perceive conditions, reason over context, execute approved=
=0Aworkflows, interact with other agents, applications and operational syst=
ems, and=0Averify results.=E2=80=9D=0A=0ARuss Ford=0A=0APresident, Projects=
 and Automation Solutions, Honeywell Technologies =C2=A0=C2=B7=C2=A0 BizTec=
h=0AMagazine, August 2026=0A[https://biztechmagazine.com/article/2026/08/ai=
-agents-factory-floor-moving-copilots-closed-loop-decision-making]=0A=0AFor=
d names the finish line directly. An agent perceives the operating conditio=
n,=0Areasons over the context it has, executes through an approved workflow=
,=0Ainteracts with the systems and people in that workflow, and verifies wh=
at=0Ahappened next. The distinction between assistance and operating delive=
ry is=0Awhether the system can move through the workflow and confirm the re=
sult.=0A=0AThat last step changes everything. A fluent recommendation is no=
t the finish=0Aline. The workflow must carry the output into an approved pr=
ocess and confirm=0Awhat happened next. If an agent recommends a production=
 resequence and nobody=0Averifies whether the resequence was loaded into th=
e ERP and did not break=0Adownstream commitments, the recommendation has no=
 consequence.=0A=0AThe takeaway=0A=0AThe test of whether an AI workflow is =
operating or advisory is whether it=0Averifies what happened next. Verify m=
eans the workflow confirms the artifact was=0Areceived, acted on, the actio=
n succeeded or failed, and the fallback executed if=0Ait did.=0A=0A=C2=A0=
=0A=0ATHE STACK=0A=0A=0AThe Seven-Field AI Output Delivery Contract.=0A=0AD=
efine what finished means before an AI response enters the handoff.=0A=0AA =
one-page specification that tells the workflow what the next person must=0A=
receive and how to act on it.=0A=0AMany AI workflows stop at a plausible an=
swer. The operation still needs a=0Aschedule, disposition record, supplier =
exception, work instruction, or system=0Atransaction that can move into the=
 real process. The boundary between assistance=0Aand delivery is whether th=
e workflow can move the artifact through an approved=0Aprocess with a clear=
 owner, acceptance rule, and fallback.=0A=0AThe seven-field contract define=
s what finished means for one bounded AI=0Aworkflow. It tells you what the =
next person must receive, what format makes it=0Ausable, what evidence trav=
els with it, where it goes, who accepts or rejects it,=0Awhat the pass cond=
ition is, and what happens if it fails. The tool lets you=0Aassess one work=
flow against these fields, see what is missing or not tested, and=0Awalk aw=
ay with a specification the team can use to build or govern that=0Aworkflow=
.=0A=0AThe seven fields=0A=0A=C2=B7 Decision or action. The exact operatio=
nal move the output must enable.=0A=0A=C2=B7 Required artifact. The deliver=
able the next person can use without rebuilding=0Ait.=0A=0A=C2=B7 Required =
format. The template, fields, file type, or system shape that makes=0Athe a=
rtifact usable.=0A=0A=C2=B7 Source evidence. The records, constraints, refe=
rences, and change history that=0Amust travel with the result.=0A=0A=C2=B7 =
Destination. The queue, record, inbox, system, or workspace where the deliv=
ery=0Abecomes actionable.=0A=0A=C2=B7 Accountable owner. The role that acce=
pts, rejects, acts, and resolves=0Aexceptions.=0A=0A=C2=B7 Acceptance test =
and fallback. The pass condition, rejection path, safe=0Afallback, escalati=
on trigger, and stop condition.=0A=0AHow the assessment works=0A=0A1. Choos=
e one bounded AI workflow and name the next person or system that must=0Aac=
t.=0A=0A2. Rate each of the seven fields as Not defined, Partly defined, or=
 Defined and=0Atested, then add any useful notes.=0A=0A3. The tool scores t=
he handoff, flags missing critical fields, identifies the=0Aweakest field, =
and recommends the first repair.=0A=0A4. You copy or print the completed co=
ntract and give it to the workflow owner or=0Abuilder before the next pilot=
 or automation change.=0A=0A5. After a controlled test, the team updates th=
e contract with any exception,=0Aownership, evidence, or fallback requireme=
nt the test exposed.=0A=0AAn illustrative example: production scheduling=0A=
=0ABefore the contract:=0A=0AThe AI recommends a production resequence. The=
 planner receives the=0Arecommendation and still has to rebuild the work. T=
hey extract it into a=0Aspreadsheet, check it against open orders, flag whi=
ch customers get impacted,=0Arebuild the constraint logic to make sure noth=
ing is missed, create a change=0Asummary for the finance team, and verify i=
t will load into the ERP without=0Aerrors. The recommendation was useful, b=
ut the workflow is not closed until the=0Aplanner has rebuilt the work by h=
and.=0A=0AAfter the contract:=0A=0AThe workflow delivers an import-ready sc=
hedule. The artifact includes the=0Areordered operations, a change summary =
for each affected order with reason=0Acodes, the source constraints, custom=
er impact flagged by order, ERP-format=0Aexport ready to load, and a pre-lo=
ad validation showing pass or fail. If=0Avalidation fails, the fallback is =
to retain the last approved schedule and=0Aescalate to the planning manager=
. The planner receives a complete, verified=0Aartifact and makes the accep=
tance decision. The hidden work is now visible and=0Ahappens in the workflo=
w, not in the planner's inbox.=0A=0ANote. The above is an illustrative exam=
ple of how the contract clarifies the=0Ahandoff. It is not a NIST case stud=
y, a completed client engagement, or proof=0Athat the recommendation is cor=
rect.=0A=0AThe tool runs in the browser. It does not push anything into an =
ERP or any other=0Asystem. Its deliverable is the completed specification y=
ou can copy or print and=0Atake to the responsible team.=0A=0AThe value is =
not the score by itself. The value is the completed specification=0Ayou wal=
k away with. Use it to confirm what finished means for that workflow,=0Ahan=
d it to the process owner or IT team, use it as the requirements handoff fo=
r=0Aa pilot or build, or attach it to a build ticket so the output is revie=
wed=0Aagainst the same contract after a controlled test.=0A=0AOpen the 7-fi=
eld delivery contract and run it on one workflow=0A[https://www.colonyspark=
.com/tools/ai-delivery-contract/=3Futm_source=3Doperations_brief&utm_mediu=
m=3Demail&utm_campaign=3Dai_delivery_contract&utm_content=3Dstack_text_link=
].=0AAn Operations Brief field tool, powered by Colony Spark.=0A=0AThe hand=
off is finished when the next person can act without rebuilding the=0Awork.=
